@node CMacs Journal Restore
@chapter CMacs Journal Restore: Put Me Back Where I Was

@cindex journal restore
@cindex checkpoints
@cindex restoring a working context
@cindex put me back where I was

@code{cmacs-journal-restore} rebuilds the working context of a past
moment from the activity journal (@pxref{CMacs Journal}).  You name the
moment---``an hour ago'', ``Tuesday 3pm'', ``before lunch'', or a line
picked in the journal---and it shows a @dfn{plan}: the files, pages,
applications and layout of that moment.  Nothing changes until you
confirm the plan.

@lisp
(cmacs-journal-mode 1)             ; the journal: what had the focus
(cmacs-journal-checkpoint-mode 1)  ; checkpoints: what was open, where
@end lisp

Then @kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-restore @key{RET} an hour ago @key{RET}}.

Four things about it, stated plainly:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Applications are started again, not resumed.}  A restore can
start a program on the tag it was on and put its window back.  What was
open inside the program---documents, tabs, scroll positions, unsaved
work---is that program's business and is not restored.  A page is
loaded again, not resumed.  The plan says this on every item it applies
to.
@item
@strong{Nothing happens before you confirm.}  Building and showing a
plan reads the journal and looks at what is running.  It visits no
file, starts no program and moves no window.
@item
@strong{It is human-only.}  A restore launches programs, so there is no
brigade tool, no MCP tool and no D-Bus method for it, and the command
refuses to run for an agent or a remote caller.
@item
@strong{It can only put back what the journal was allowed to keep.}
Checkpoints go through the same privacy filters as everything else in
the journal.  A window a rule excludes is not in any checkpoint, so it
is not in any plan.
@end itemize

It is part of the journal: built with @option{--with-cmacs-journal},
pure Elisp.  Placing and starting windows needs gowl; files, pages and
the Emacs window layout work on any cmacs.

