@node Journal Checkpoints
@section Checkpoints

The journal's spans say what had the @emph{focus}.  They do not say
what else was open or how it was laid out.  A @dfn{checkpoint} is that
missing half: a journal event of kind @code{checkpoint}, written from a
timer, whose @code{detail} field is a JSON document.

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Member @tab What it holds
@item @code{frames} @tab Per Emacs frame, the window tree as data: splits, their proportions, and each window's buffer, file, mode and line
@item @code{buffers} @tab The open file-visiting buffers, shown or not (at most @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-max-buffers})
@item @code{urls} @tab The pages open in gsurf buffers
@item @code{clients} @tab gowl's windows: app id, title, tags, monitor, floating and geometry, pid, command line
@item @code{monitors} @tab Per output: the tags in view, the layout symbol, which one was focused
@end multitable

The window tree is plain data---never a window-configuration
object---so it survives a restart and can be read with @command{jq}.

@subheading When one is written

@code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-mode} starts a timer and nothing else.
Every @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-interval} seconds it looks at the
arrangement, and writes a checkpoint only when all of these hold:

@itemize @bullet
@item
the journal is recording (@code{cmacs-journal-mode} on, not paused,
session not locked, filters usable);
@item
the arrangement differs from the last one written, or the last one is
older than @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-max-age};
@item
something is left after filtering.
@end itemize

Point moving and a window retitling itself are not changes: they are in
a checkpoint but do not cause one.  Otherwise a terminal that puts the
running command in its title would produce a checkpoint every interval.

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-checkpoint-take} writes one now.

@subheading Filters

A checkpoint is many events' worth of names, so @strong{every entry is
filtered on its own}.  Each buffer, page, window and monitor is turned
into the event the journal would have recorded for it, handed to the
filter engine, and the entry is built from what comes back:

@itemize @bullet
@item
An @code{exclude} rule removes the entry.  An excluded window is not
blanked in the tree, it is @emph{absent}: its sibling takes its place,
and nothing counts it or records that a rule matched.
@item
@code{drop}, @code{replace} and @code{hash} do to the entry's fields
what they do to a span's.  A hashed file name is in the checkpoint as
its hash, and a restore lists it as withheld.
@item
A window's command line is filtered as the entry's @code{detail} field.
Separately, a command line naming a URL that a @code{url} rule would not
record (@samp{firefox https://bank.example/}) is withheld whole, since
no @code{url} rule would otherwise look there.
@item
The finished event goes through the filters once more as a whole, so a
rule on @samp{kind: checkpoint} turns checkpoints off.
@end itemize

It @strong{fails closed} exactly as the journal does: with an unusable
rule set the journal is not recording, so no checkpoint is taken, and
the filter engine refuses every entry in that state anyway.

@subheading Storage, retention, forgetting, LQL

Checkpoints live in the journal's own day files, written through the
journal's own queue, so everything the journal does to an event it does
to a checkpoint:

@itemize @bullet
@item
same directory and modes (0700 / 0600);
@item
@code{cmacs-journal-retention-days} prunes them with their day;
@item
@code{cmacs-journal-forget} deletes them: by time range like any event,
and by regexp when the regexp matches anything in the document (a
buffer name, a title, a command);
@item
the LQL @code{activity} source has them as rows whose @code{kind} is
@code{checkpoint}, with the focused monitor in @code{monitor}, its tags
in @code{tags} and the document in @code{detail}, under the same agent
gate.  A query over attention that should not see them filters on
@code{kind}.
@end itemize

Reports ignore them: they have no duration, and @code{checkpoint} is
not in @code{cmacs-journal-report-kinds}.

@subheading The command line

On Linux the command line comes from @file{/proc/@var{pid}/cmdline},
read once per process, shell-quoted so @code{gowl-spawn} reads back the
same argument list.  A Flatpak's process sees its own @file{/app}, which
is no command on the host, so for those the recorded command is
@samp{flatpak run @var{app-id}} when the app id looks like one.
@code{cmacs-journal-restore-app-commands} overrides what was recorded,
per app id.  Set @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-command-lines} to
@code{nil} to record none; a restore can then only move windows that
are already running.

@node Journal Restore Times
@section Naming the Moment

@code{cmacs-journal-restore-parse-time} reads a phrase.  Every
ambiguity resolves to the @strong{most recent past moment}.

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem Phrase @tab Means
@item @samp{now} @tab Now
@item @samp{10 minutes ago}, @samp{an hour ago}, @samp{half an hour ago} @tab That long ago
@item @samp{an hour and a half ago}, @samp{a couple of hours ago} @tab 90 minutes, two hours
@item @samp{2 days ago}, @samp{2 days ago at 3pm} @tab The same clock time two days ago; or that day at 15:00
@item @samp{3pm}, @samp{3:30pm}, @samp{15:00} @tab Today's if it has passed, otherwise yesterday's
@item @samp{3}, @samp{9:15} @tab No am/pm: whichever of the two happened last
@item @samp{noon}, @samp{this morning}, @samp{before lunch}, @samp{last night} @tab A named part of the day (@code{cmacs-journal-restore-day-parts})
@item @samp{today}, @samp{yesterday}, @samp{the day before yesterday} @tab A day with no time: how that day ended
@item @samp{Tuesday}, @samp{Tuesday 3pm}, @samp{3pm on Tuesday} @tab The most recent Tuesday
@item @samp{last Friday 9am} @tab A Friday before today
@item @samp{2026-11-18}, @samp{2026-11-18 15:00}, with @samp{Z} or an offset @tab ISO 8601; a date alone is the end of that day
@item @samp{18 nov 15:00} @tab Whatever @code{parse-time-string} reads as a date
@end multitable

The rules behind the table:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A time with no day is today's if it has happened, and yesterday's if
not.
@item
A weekday is the most recent one.  Today counts when the time named has
passed; a weekday @emph{alone}, on that weekday, is last week's.
@item
A day with no time is the last second of that day (capped at now), so
``yesterday'' restores the last arrangement of yesterday.
@item
``before lunch'' is the moment lunch started.  A restore takes the
arrangement recorded at or before its moment, so that is what was open
going into lunch.
@item
A moment still in the future is not a moment to restore: the parser
returns @code{nil} and the command says so.
@item
Number words work (@samp{three thirty p.m.}), for speech.
@end itemize

@node Journal Restore Plan
@section The Plan

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-restore} asks for the moment and shows the plan
in @file{*cmacs-journal-restore*}.  The plan is built from the
@strong{nearest checkpoint at or before the moment} (looking back up to
@code{cmacs-journal-restore-lookback}) plus the focus spans recorded
between that checkpoint and the moment---things opened after the
arrangement was last written down.  The header says when the checkpoint
is from.

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .7
@headitem Section @tab Items
@item Emacs buffers and files @tab Each file to visit.  Remote files are listed but off
@item Pages @tab Each URL to open again
@item Applications @tab Per window: already in place, move to its tag, or start it on its tag
@item Layout and focus @tab The Emacs window tree; per monitor the tags to view and the layout; what to focus
@item Cannot be restored @tab Everything that will not be put back, each with its reason
@end multitable

@table @kbd
@item @key{RET}
@itemx @key{SPC}
@itemx t
Tick or untick the item at point.
@item a
@itemx u
Tick, or untick, every item in the section at point.
@item @key{TAB}
Fold or unfold the section.
@item c
@itemx C-c C-c
Restore what is ticked (asks once).
@item g
Build the plan again.
@item T
Another moment.
@item q
Leave; nothing was changed.
@end table

@subheading What cannot be restored, and why

The last section is not a footnote; it is half of what the plan is for.

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Listed as @tab Reason
@item A file @tab It no longer exists
@item ``a file'', ``a page'', ``a window'' @tab Its name was withheld by a journal filter (hashed or dropped)
@item A buffer @tab It visited no file and is gone (@file{*compilation*}, a REPL)
@item An application @tab It is not running and no command line was recorded
@item An application @tab It was embedded in an Emacs window, or on no tag (a scratchpad)
@item An application @tab gowl is not running here
@item Other Emacs frames @tab Frames are not recreated; their files are still reopened
@item A monitor @tab It is not connected now
@item A layout @tab Its symbol has no name in @code{cmacs-journal-restore-layout-names}
@item Windows, without a checkpoint @tab The journal has a title, not a command or a place
@end multitable

Remote files are a special case: they are in the files section,
@emph{unticked}, and are not touched unless you tick them.  Building
the plan never asks Tramp anything.

Without any checkpoint (the mode was off) the plan is built from focus
spans alone: files and pages that had the focus near the moment, and a
note saying to turn on @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-mode}.

@node Journal Restore Execution
@section What a Restore Does

In this order; a step that fails is written to the plan's log and the
rest go on.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Files.}  Each ticked file is visited.  A file this restore
opened is moved to its recorded line; one that was already open is left
where you have it.  A file that vanished since the plan was drawn is
skipped.
@item
@strong{Pages.}  Opened in gsurf buffers when gsurf is built
(@code{IS-CMACS-GSURF}), otherwise handed to @code{browse-url}.  A page
recorded in a gsurf container opens in the default container, and the
plan says so.
@item
@strong{The Emacs window tree.}  The selected frame's windows are
replaced by the recorded tree, split in the recorded proportions, each
window at its line.  If the frame is too small for all of it, what fits
is built and the log says so.
@item
@strong{Applications.}  A running window that matches a recorded one
(same app id, the same title preferred, each window claimed once) is
moved to its recorded tags.  One that is not running is started with
@code{gowl-spawn} and registered with @code{gowl-pretag-pid}, so its
window maps directly on its tag and monitor instead of flashing on the
one in view.  A window that floated floats again at its recorded
geometry.
@item
@strong{Views.}  Each monitor views its recorded tags and gets its
recorded layout, the focused monitor last.
@item
@strong{Focus.}  The window or buffer that had the focus at the
moment---from the span around it, else from the checkpoint---is
focused.
@end enumerate

@strong{Nothing waits.}  A started program's window is looked for from
a timer every @code{cmacs-journal-restore-poll-interval} seconds; when
it appears it is placed (and focused, if it was the focused one), and
after @code{cmacs-journal-restore-app-timeout} seconds without one the
log says it did not appear.  Under @command{emacs --gowl} Emacs is the
compositor, and waiting for a window would be waiting for itself.

Tags are bitmasks in gowl (tag 1 is bit 0).  A checkpoint stores tag
@emph{numbers}; the restore converts.

Layouts are best effort: the compositor reports a monitor's layout
@emph{symbol} (@samp{[]=}), two layouts can share one, and
@code{cmacs-journal-restore-layout-names} maps symbols to the layout to
ask for.

Running windows are matched by app id and are moved between tags, not
between monitors.

@node Journal Restore Doors
@section Other Ways In

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{From the journal.}  In @code{cmacs-journal-at} output, @kbd{R}
on a line shows the plan for the last moment that span had the focus.
In a @code{cmacs-journal-report} by day, @kbd{C-c C-r} on a row shows
the plan for the end of that day.  Both are
@code{cmacs-journal-restore-at-point}, and both only show the plan.
@item
@strong{By voice.}  ``put me back where I was an hour ago'', ``put me
back where I was Tuesday 3pm'', ``restore my desktop from yesterday''.
The plan buffer comes up first, always; then the question is asked the
voice way (say yes or no, or press @kbd{y}/@kbd{n}).  Only a yes runs
it.  With voice confirmations turned off
(@code{cmacs-voice-confirm-destructive} @code{nil}) nothing is run from
speech at all: the plan stays up for you to confirm by hand.
@item
@strong{Not by agents.}  There is deliberately no tool for this.
@code{cmacs-journal-restore} and @code{cmacs-journal-restore-execute}
signal an error inside a brigade tool call or over MCP, D-Bus, emacsctl
and bacon, and a plan built there is empty because the journal returns
no events to those callers.
@end itemize

@node Journal Restore Options
@section Commands and Options

@table @code
@item cmacs-journal-checkpoint-mode
Global minor mode: record arrangements.  Off by default.
@item cmacs-journal-checkpoint-take
Write a checkpoint now.
@item cmacs-journal-restore
Show the plan for a moment.
@item cmacs-journal-restore-at-point
Show the plan for the journal line at point.
@item cmacs-journal-restore-confirm
In the plan buffer: do it.
@end table

From Lisp: @code{(cmacs-journal-restore-parse-time @var{text} &optional
@var{now})} returns seconds or @code{nil};
@code{(cmacs-journal-restore-plan @var{time})} returns the plan as a
plist (@code{:time}, @code{:checkpoint}, @code{:items}, @code{:notes},
@code{:log}) without changing anything;
@code{(cmacs-journal-restore-execute @var{plan})} carries it out and
fills @code{:log}; @code{(cmacs-journal-checkpoint-decode @var{event})}
turns a checkpoint event into its document.

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .15 .4
@headitem Option @tab Default @tab Meaning
@item @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-interval} @tab 120 @tab Seconds between looks at the arrangement
@item @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-max-age} @tab 3600 @tab Write an unchanged arrangement again after this long
@item @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-max-buffers} @tab 100 @tab File buffers listed per checkpoint
@item @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-line-limit} @tab 2000000 @tab Larger buffers get no line number
@item @code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-command-lines} @tab @code{t} @tab Record command lines from @file{/proc}
@item @code{cmacs-journal-restore-day-parts} @tab (see above) @tab ``morning'', ``lunch'', @dots{} and their clock times
@item @code{cmacs-journal-restore-lookback} @tab 7 days @tab How far before a moment to look for its checkpoint
@item @code{cmacs-journal-restore-app-timeout} @tab 30 @tab Seconds to wait for a started program's window
@item @code{cmacs-journal-restore-poll-interval} @tab 0.5 @tab Seconds between looks for those windows
@item @code{cmacs-journal-restore-app-commands} @tab @code{nil} @tab @code{(@var{app-id-regexp} . @var{command})}; a @code{nil} command means never start it
@item @code{cmacs-journal-restore-layout-names} @tab (shipped) @tab Layout symbol to layout name
@end multitable

@node Journal Restore Internals
@section Internals

@subheading Why a journal event

A checkpoint could have been a sibling file.  It is a journal event
because @code{cmacs-journal-forget}, retention, the file modes, the
atomic rewrite, the agent gate and the LQL source all operate on the
day files.  A second file would need a second copy of each, and the
copy that lags is the one that keeps what you asked to have deleted.

@subheading The three rules, here

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Nothing runs on a hook.}  A checkpoint is taken from a timer,
which may read @file{/proc} and count lines (never in a buffer over
@code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-line-limit}).  The gowl DEFUNs it calls
are the list and info ones (@code{gowl-list-clients},
@code{gowl-client-info}, @code{gowl-client-pid},
@code{gowl-list-monitors}, @code{gowl-monitor-info},
@code{gowl-focused-client}), which take the gowl lock themselves and
return.  The timer function is wrapped in @code{condition-case} and
never signals.
@item
@strong{Filters before the queue.}  Entries are filtered one at a time
before the document exists, and the document once more before it is
queued.
@item
@strong{Fail closed.}  No usable rule set, no checkpoint.
@end itemize

A restore's own compositor calls---@code{gowl-spawn},
@code{gowl-pretag-pid}, @code{gowl-set-tags}, @code{gowl-view-tags},
@code{gowl-set-layout}, @code{gowl-focus-client},
@code{gowl-set-client-floating},
@code{gowl-client-set-geometry}---are made from a command or from the
poll timer, on the main thread, each inside its own
@code{condition-case}.

